foreign  [Music]  briefing for Friday December 9th I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  have you ever gotten in a car and looked  up directions on Google Maps only to  then check Waze to see if it has a  faster route  well you might not realize the two  services are actually both owned by  Google the units have worked mostly  separately since Google bought ways in  2013. but this week Google's parent  company alphabet said it plans to  combine them what's behind the change  and how will employees and users be  affected joining me to talk through that  is wsj reporter miles Krupa who covers  all things alphabet the high miles  thanks for joining me thanks Zoe so what  do we know about the reasons for  combining maps and ways  so for a long time maps and ways have  basically operated independently  and right now we're in this moment where  the economy is getting worse Google is  facing more pressure as advertising  growth slows down and recently the  Google CEO Sundar pichai has been  talking about how Google might need to  reduce overlap in some products across  the company for instance in the past  Google had Google Play Music and also  YouTube music and they sort of brought  those two things together so ways and  Maps sort of seemed like a natural area  of overlap to reduce Google said some of  the benefits from this move will be that  the teams can work together on their map  making efforts some of these efforts  were being duplicated across both ways  and Google Maps and it doesn't make  sense for those to be kept separate so  you could see it improving efficiency  and potentially also improving costs  down the line if they're able to find  technical costs they can cut by being  able to centralize some of the stuff in  one organization  what is this going to mean for the  people who work in each of these units  then so Google said there won't be any  layoffs associated with this move but  ways workers will be going to a new  organization led by this executive Chris  Phillips who leads Maps Earth street  view under this organization called Geo  so these people who used to work fairly  separately on two products that were in  some ways competing under the same roof  are now going to be I guess in the same  room together not just under the same  roof the idea is that they'll be  collaborating more on things like  navigation how to improve the user  experience on both apps and how to learn  from both apps but I have to think it's  going to be just a little awkward since  they're planning to keep ways as its own  Standalone app and clearly there are  people who are devoted to one or the  other you mentioned that people are  devoted to one or the other I mean there  is somewhat of a divide between people  who use Google Maps to get around and  people who swear by ways to get around  so what is this combination going to  mean for users of these apps  so far I think it will mean very little  for the users they plan to keep ways as  a separate app a separate brand you know  it will be interesting to see how the  ways Community reacts to this because  Waze maintains a very active community  of basically unpaid Freelancers who  crowdsource data about traffic and are  constantly updating the ways navigation  maps so I'm sure people in the community  will have thoughts about these team  members now working with Google Maps  let's take a step back for a second  Google acquired ways in 2013. can you  tell us a little bit about that  combination over time  sure so as you said Google acquired ways  in 2013 for about 1.1 billion dollars uh  it was a big acquisition for the company  at the time it drew some scrutiny from  Regulators worried that Google was  basically eliminating its biggest  competitor in maps But ultimately it was  cleared  for the longest time Waze was led by  this CEO gnome Barton who was sort of  the face of the company the charismatic  leader and based on my understanding he  did a good job of sort of protecting  ways within Google and trying to keep it  as an independent thing in 2021 he  actually left the company and later said  that he thought Waze probably could have  grown faster as an independent company  and kind of hit out at the Google  bureaucracy they brought in a new CEO  last year Neha Parikh plans to exit the  organization after a transition period  the reason Google gave is that because  Waze is no longer an independent  organization it does not need its own  CEO what's still unclear is whether Neha  will stay with Google or alphabet in  some role so we'll have to see where she  lands okay so that's the change to Waze  and to Google Maps but as you you  mentioned this is part of a bigger  strategy for alphabet to deal with cost  cutting I mean do we expect to see more  steps like this at the company  yeah I think we will see more stuff like  this we've already seen Google kill  stadia which was its Cloud streaming  gaming service we've seen it make cuts  at an internal incubator called area 120  . so far it's sort of small stuff around  the edges and I think we're still  waiting for a really big cut but this is  maybe a sign that Sundar and the rest of  the Google leadership is looking for  areas where there are redundancies and  where they want to reduce those  what about Investors how are they  reacting to these steps  so far Google has been much more  cautious than almost all the other big  tech companies and cutting costs and  especially cutting head count so I think  there's a general sentiment among  investors that  the head count of Google is too high  there was an activist hedge fund TCI  that wrote a letter to that effect last  month and I spoke to other shareholders  who basically felt like TCI was sort of  saying the quiet part out loud so I  think what investors really want to see  is a headcount reduction and to be frank  that's just very ungoogly so I think  Sundar and the rest of Google leadership  is grappling with weather and how they  could ever do something like that all  right that's our reporter miles Krupa  thanks for joining us miles thanks Zoe  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our producer is Julie Chang we  had editorial support from Falana  Patterson our executive producer is  Chris Tinsley and I'm your host Zoe  Thomas thanks for listening and have a  wonderful weekend  [Music]  [Music]

